e/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1956x1500+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F9f%2Fc8%2F33103fa7402ba264b48ddc1ee50e%2Ftomkins.jpg" class="img" alt="A bald, bearded man in a light winter coat with a hoodie underneath stands in a large lobby room." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins, a Democrat, has won the race for governor of Alaska and will replace the outgoing Republican. Here he's seen at a polling station at the University of Alaska Anchorage on Election Day.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Eric Stone/Alaska Public Media                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Democrat Jonathan Kreiss-Tomkins, a former state representative from the small island community of Sitka, won the race for Alaska governor, according to a race call by the Associated Press. At 37, he'll be&lt;a href="https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/elections/2026-11-13/alaska-elects-democrat-jonathan-kreiss-tomkins-governor" target="_blank"&gt; the youngest governor in state history.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Kreiss-Tomkins will replace outgoing Gov. Mike Dunleavy, a term-limited Republican who has been governor since 2018. The state's last Democratic governor was Tony Knowles, who left office in 2002.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Kreiss-Tomkins, known by his initials JKT, made his "high-energy, bipartisan, problem-solving approach" a centerpiece of his campaign.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;He's said he plans to build an administration of Democrats, Republicans and independents and described himself at a debate as a "man of the middle." He was endorsed by three former governors — a Democrat, a Republican and an independent — along with Republican Sen. Lisa Murkowski.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Alaskans have had enough of zero-sum partisan politics, he said in an interview just before voting at a precinct in Anchorage on Election Day.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"I strongly believe people just want things to work, and want people to work together," Kreiss-Tomkins said.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-151719-100" class="bucketwrap statichtml"&gt;             &lt;p data-pym-loader data-child-src="https://apps.npr.org/2026-election-results/race-embed.html?embedded=true&amp;stateAbbrev=AK&amp;race=2010" id="responsive-embed-AK-2010"&gt; Loading... &lt;/p&gt; &lt;script src="https://pym.nprapps.org/npr-pym-loader.v2.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESG-S1-151719-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP STATICHTML" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The AP race call came after over a &lt;a href="https://alaskapublic.org/news/politics/elections/2026-11-02/alaska-counts-ballots-slowly-heres-what-to-expect-on-election-night-and-beyond" target="_blank"&gt;week of counting votes&lt;/a&gt;, including those coming in by mail and from remote parts of the state. In the ranked-choice system the state uses, he faced three Republican candidates, including one backed by President Trump. Winning requires getting a majority of the votes.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Voters were also facing a question on the ballot about whether to scrap that unique, ranked-choice system. That proposal failed, according to another AP race call, keeping the system in place.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;With Kreiss-Tomkins' win and others in the midterms, Democrats will hold 30 governors' offices across the country, up from 24 now.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Kreiss-Tomkins will take office in a state where voters have loudly complained that things aren't working as they should.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Rising costs and relatively flat state funding have forced schools to close and class sizes to rise. Already-high costs for food, fuel and housing have risen sharply, and a looming shortage of easily accessible natural gas near the state's population centers is threatening to push prices higher. And Alaskans have been migrating out of the state for more than a decade.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The Republicans in the race were Trump-backed business owner Bernadette Wilson, former Anchorage Mayor Dave Bronson and former Alaska Attorney General Treg Taylor.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Kreiss-Tomkins is set to take office at noon Dec. 7.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div class="hr"&gt;&lt;hr /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://alaskapublic.org/people/eric-stone" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eric Stone&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; covers state government for &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://alaskapublic.org/" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Alaska Public Media&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <category>         Elections     </category>
      <category>2026 midterms</category>
    </item>
