emplate="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2511x1674+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F8a%2F6d%2Fbb7e77884e4fb7331a20a24d8a8a%2Fap26230766602771.jpg" class="img" alt="An election worker sorts mail-in ballots at the Miami-Dade County Supervisor of Elections Office in Doral for Florida’s primary election in August." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 An election worker sorts mail-in ballots at the Miami-Dade County Supervisor of Elections Office in Doral for Florida's primary election in August.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Lynne Sladky/AP                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Voting for this year's midterm elections just concluded, but Republican officials in a number of states are already looking to shape how votes are cast in 2028.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Twelve GOP state attorneys general are pushing to revive the president's bid for mail-in voting restrictions in time for the 2028 election.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;On Thursday, they &lt;a href="https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/28895444-dscc-v-trump-2026-11-13-notice-of-appeal-by-intervener-defendants/" target="_blank"&gt;started the process&lt;/a&gt; of appealing &lt;a href="https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/28621082-dscc-v-trump-2026-09-13-memorandum-opinion/" target="_blank"&gt;a September order&lt;/a&gt; by U.S. District Judge Carl Nichols, one of the two judges who blocked the U.S. Postal Service from carrying out a plan released in response to an &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/03/31/nx-s1-5508948/trump-voter-list-mail-ballots-executive-order" target="_blank"&gt;executive order by President Trump&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The Republican state attorneys general represent Alabama, Florida, Indiana, Kansas, Louisiana, Missouri, Montana, Nebraska, Oklahoma, South Carolina, South Dakota and Texas. It's not clear, however, how long some of these states will continue this case, given that Democrats won up and down the ballot in the midterms. In Texas and Kansas, Democratic candidates are set to take control of their states' attorney general's offices in January.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;div id="resnx-s1-6040409-100" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-6040409-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;In September, the Supreme Court &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/14/nx-s1-5962190/supreme-court-mail-in-voting-trump" target="_blank"&gt;refused to pause&lt;/a&gt; a similar ruling by U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani, who is based in Boston. That order stopped USPS from working on its revised rule for federal ballot mail, but it only applied to this year's midterm elections.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Nichols' preliminary injunction blocks USPS from implementing the plan for all federal elections, including the 2028 race.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In a &lt;a href="https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/74701505/182/state-of-california-v-united-states-postal-service/" target="_blank"&gt;court filing&lt;/a&gt;, Justice Department attorneys confirmed the Trump administration is not appealing Nichols' ruling. The appeal deadline was Thursday.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The blocked rule — which the Postal Service released in late August — would require states to turn over lists of voters' names and addresses in order to have them delivered by USPS.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-6040409-101" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-6040409-101" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Nichols, a Washington, D.C.-based judge who was nominated by Trump, found that the mailing agency likely has no power to impose that requirement on states.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"As the Government concedes, the Postal Service could issue (and enforce) the Rule only if Congress gave it the authority to do so, but here no statute grants the Postal Service the power to issue key parts of the Rule," Nichols wrote.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The administration's earlier push to put these new restrictions in place for the midterms alarmed many election officials, who were concerned about last-minute changes causing major disruptions to the tens of millions who regularly vote by mail. Some voters with disabilities and those in places without polling sites, they feared, could have lost the ability to exercise their right to cast a ballot.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/01/nx-s1-5950869/trump-mail-in-voting-usps" target="_blank"&gt;whistleblower report&lt;/a&gt; claimed the online system officials would need to use to follow USPS' revised ballot requirements had not been properly tested and could have resulted in many eligible voters not getting their mail-in ballots in time or at all.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;While the Supreme Court ultimately stopped implementation of the USPS rule for the midterms, Justices Samuel Alito and Clarence Thomas dissented from that ruling.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Fellow conservative Justice Brett Kavanaugh said in a concurring opinion that he believed state and local election officials did not have enough time to "reasonably implement the rule" this time. But Kavanaugh noted that "there is at least a fair prospect that the final rule falls within the Postal Service's statutory authority."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/795948473/benjamin-swasey" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Benjamin Swasey&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
